{
  "note": "Physical facts about the Moon and the eight planets for the body cards (apps/web/src/bodyFacts.ts). Each value names the page or kernel it was read from (`sources`): the Moon, Earth and Mars on 2026-11-11; Mercury, Venus, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune and every obliquityDeg on 2026-11-15, when the Moon, Earth and Mars sheets still carried the update dates below. Radii are NASA's volumetric mean radii; pck00011.tpc agrees (the Moon's 1737.4 km sphere; Earth and Mars as ellipsoids whose mean radii are 6371.01 km and 3389.53 km). Each GM is the body's own value in gm_de440.tpc (BODY199_GM to BODY899_GM, and BODY301_GM for the Moon), never a system barycentre's. rotationH is the sidereal rotation period as NASA prints it, negative for a retrograde spin (Venus, Uranus); the four giants' sheets mark theirs as System III (Jupiter, Saturn) or magnetic coordinates from Voyager 2 (Uranus, Neptune). solarDayH is the sheet's length of day; the Moon's is its synodic period, 29.53 days, as hours. obliquityDeg is the obliquity to orbit. Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune have no solid surface (`surface`: none): their sheets give the temperature at the 1 bar level (`levelBar`, `temperatureK.atLevelK`) and the surface pressure only as '>>1000 bars', so surfacePressureMbar is null, and equatorialRadiusKm is the equatorial radius at 1 bar, the radius NASA's printed escape velocity uses. The Moon's and Mercury's sheets give only trace exosphere pressures (the Moon 3 x 10^-15 bar at night, Mercury under about 5 x 10^-15 bar), so their surfacePressureMbar is null too; Venus's 92 bar is stored as 92000 mbar. Mercury's meanK is the sheet's average temperature; its sunward-side range, 590 to 725 K, is not a day-to-night range and is not stored. Gravity, escape speed, sunlight strength and signal delay are not stored: the app works them out from these numbers and the planets table.",
